Keep recording through a mute while the echo canceller is transparent, for a short while

Patch 0010 stops the audio device's recording when the last stream sending sound goes (a
microphone turned off in a call), and at the unmute starts it again and restarts AEC3 for
recording, keeping its transparent mode (with a headset: that there is no echo). While
recording is stopped AEC3 hears nothing of the room, so nothing it keeps can be checked
until the unmute: a headset taken off for speakers with no change of devices anyone could
tell keeps the headset's transparent mode and lets the speakers' echo through after the
unmute (0010: -15 dBFS, heard 1.1 s), and each stop and start of the device is one more
chance for the sound server or the driver to move things (patch 0011: a Bluetooth headset
switching profile as recording stops and starts, its sink and source removed and added).

Now, while AEC3 is in its transparent mode, the microphone stays open through the first
moments of a mute, up to a cap: FOGATA_A1_HOLD_MS, an environment variable read once when
the AudioState is made (std::getenv; rtc_base has no helper for it), in milliseconds;
unset, 0 or not a positive number: no hold at all, as without this patch. A lab knob, to
try 200 to 300 ms. When the last sending stream goes in a call that goes on playing (the
same test as 0010's stopped_in_call_), and the audio processing says its echo controller
was transparent after the last recorded frame, AudioState does not stop recording and
marks it held ("recording held" in the log). The audio transport already processes what
is recorded with no stream, in the last streams' format (patch 0010), and sends it nowhere
(AudioTransportImpl; a transport given to the factory, patch 0002, has to do the same);
AEC3 goes on hearing the room. The hold ends, and recording stops as before
(stopped_in_call_ true, so the unmute is 0010's), as soon as the echo controller is no
longer transparent (an echo path found: speakers; or the controller made anew), logged
"held recording stopped (not transparent) N ms", or FOGATA_A1_HOLD_MS after the hold
began, logged "held recording stopped (cap) N ms", N being the time since the hold began
(TimeMillis). It ends too, as stopped_in_call_ does, when a stream received comes or goes,
playout or recording is turned off, or the call ends; and at a change of audio devices
(AudioDevicesChangedCount moved), after which the unmute takes the devices as changed
(patch 0016's restart for other devices, forgetting the transparent mode) even when a
device's own restart while held handled the change (patch 0014's realign keeps the
transparent mode, which a headset taken off must not). These log "held recording stopped
(<why>) N ms", or "ended" where recording goes on or the caller stopped it; one stop and
one log per hold. At an unmute with recording held nothing is started: the echo
controller is restarted for recording as at any unmute (RestartEchoControllerForRecording,
or RestartEchoControllerForOtherDevices after a change), and the hold is cleared ("held
recording ended (the microphone turned on again) N ms"). A device that stopped recording
by itself while held is started as usual.

How AEC3 tells it: EchoControl::TransparentModeActive (false unless overridden), which
EchoCanceller3 answers from its block processor, its echo remover and AecState's transparent
mode; AudioProcessingImpl stores it in an atomic after each ProcessStream, with its capture
lock held, processed or not (false with no echo controller, and false again when the
controller is made anew), and AudioProcessing::EchoControllerTransparent reads it from any
thread. AEC3 keeps updating its transparent mode while the processing's output is unused
(patch 0008: nothing sends).

Threads: everything about the hold is on the worker thread, where AudioState makes every
other call to the audio device module. At the mute it posts two delayed tasks there for
the hold, which carries an id moved on at each hold's start and end: a check every 10 ms
(it reads the atomic and the count of device changes, and stops recording there) and the
cap after FOGATA_A1_HOLD_MS. Each does nothing unless its id is the hold's, the hold goes
on and nothing sends; the unmute and every other end of the hold move the id on, so a task
already posted finds the hold over, whichever runs first on the worker: an unmute just
before the cap keeps recording and the cap does nothing; the cap just before the unmute
stops recording and the unmute starts it as 0010's. Tasks run only while the AudioState
lives (SafeTask, a PendingTaskSafetyFlag). The cap is time on the worker, not recorded
frames: it ends the hold even when the device delivers nothing, up to the worker's own
delay late. Nothing is added to the capture path but the atomic's store: a hook in
AudioTransportImpl would miss the transport the wrapper gives the factory (patch 0002),
and would have to post to the worker anyway. Recording stops up to 10 ms (and the worker's
own delay) after AEC3 leaves its transparent mode; what is recorded meanwhile is processed
and dropped as any frame with no stream. There is no hold without a task queue on the
calling thread.

The cost: with a headset the microphone stays open for up to the cap after the mute while
AEC3 is in transparent mode: the system shows it in use, the device records and the audio
processing runs for nothing sent, until the hold ends; with speakers (no transparent mode)
recording stops at the mute as before.

Measured: not yet.

Unit tests (AudioStateRecordingHoldTest, simulated clock): the cap stops the recording once,
exactly at FOGATA_A1_HOLD_MS; an unmute during the hold keeps it recording; the hold ends at the
next 10 ms poll once the echo controller is no longer transparent.

